{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And the waters split.</b> This implies down to the sea floor. And another verse says: ״the depths froze in the heart of the sea,״ which implies they were not split completely. Rather, this sea is in flood, and had the waters not split at all, Israel would have needed to toil and climb upward; and if they had split completely, they would have needed to descend to the abyss. Therefore the floodwater, which was an excess layer, split and became a wall for Israel on this side and that, and the two deeps froze — this is the meaning of ״in the heart of the sea״: just as a person's walking is at a third of his body height, so too the waters froze at a third of their volume. And when Israel crossed through the sea, they did not need to ascend or descend, for the water was level with the ground. This is what the liturgical poet expressed: ״They turned aside here and there, a third of the surging waters; the remainder served as a pavement for the soles.״ <b>And he stretched out, and he caused to go, and he turned.</b> Each of these three verses contains seventy-two letters, and from them emerges the Name of seventy-two letters, as explained in tractate Sukkah, chapter Lulav va-Aravah. And by means of it Moses split the sea. And since at first Moses applied it over the waters, therefore in the Hoshanot on the festival on which we are judged concerning water, we mention from it the first two words split into two halves, and it appears as though we mentioned all of it — and they are אני and הוא, the one being the first word and the other the thirty-seventh word."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The poet's quotation is a medieval Hebrew piyyut fragment in unpointed spelling; rendered literally. תולתא is Aramaic for a third or an added layer; here rendered 'excess layer' per context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:54.517272368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}